DeAngelis, 2012). In fact, accurate record keeping is essential for sustainability. Traditionally, even in times of declining university budgets, as
long as research funding is sustained, universities support high-performing statistical consulting centres. In addition, funding must be adequate
to support a director and provide administrative support to administer the centre, document its impacts and communicate them effectively to
various internal and external stakeholders (Vance, 2015). While the percent effort required for the director and other centre leadership and
administrative support will depend on the size and activity level of the centre, the mechanism for funding should be considered. For example,
centres could require a 25:75 split where 25% of an FTE or project cost is allotted to cover the operational costs of the centre while 75%
covers the salary and benefits of the centre employee or student. Such arrangements may differ based on whether the partner is in industry,
academia or government.
5 | DISCUSSION
By comprehensively understanding the various aspects of a financial model, statistical consulting centres can develop sustainable budgets and
other supports, price their services appropriately and deliver high-quality statistical expertise to their clients. To this end, this article detailed the
financial models of academic statistical consulting centres at three different institutions, reviewed both historical and modern perspectives on
funding considerations, outlined a robust set of potential costs and revenues of a centre and discussed points of particular importance at different
phases of a project and points in the life cycle of a centre. This overview is intended to provide statistical consulting groups interested in becom
ing either more or less formally structured with some important points of consideration.
